ENTRY            C3128   20260512   20260819   20260819       C258
SUBENT        C3128001   20260512   20260819   20260819       C258
BIB                 13         58
TITLE      A Study of the Higher Excitation Levels in 14C via the
           10Be(alpha,n) 13C Reaction in the 2.5 MeV < Ealpha <
           8.5 MeV Energy Range
AUTHOR     (J.F.Guillemette,T.N.Massey,J.E.O'Donnell,E.F.Saito,
           R.O.Lane,S.M.Grimes)
REFERENCE  (T,GUILLEMETTE,1994) Main reference.
           (J,BAP,37,1264,1992) Preliminary results.
           (C,2004SANTA,1,450,2004) Conference presentation.
           #doi:10.1063/1.1945045
INSTITUTE  (1USAOHO)
           (1USAUSA) Winthrop High School, Winthrop, Maine 0436
FACILITY   (VDGT,1USAOHO) The tandem Van de Graaff, beam swinger
           and TOF tunnel of the Ohio University Accelerator
           Laboratory.
INC-SOURCE The alpha beam was produced in a duoplasmatron,
           charge exchange, 4He- source. Beam current was from
           12 to 50 nA.
DETECTOR   (SCIN) The separate detectors: Each detector
           consisted of a 11.25 cm diameter by 5 cm thick
           cylinder filled with NE213 liquid scintillator.
SAMPLE     (4-BE-10,ENR=0.94) A 92+-20 microg/cm2 BeO target,
           enriched to 94% 10Be, was used for all cross section
           measurements.
METHOD     (TOF) The 10BeO target was attached to a target wheel
           in the center of a scattering chamber. The chamber was
           mounted in line with the rotation axis of the Swinger
           Facility so the the alpha particle beam always hit the
           target at normal incidence. The neutrons produced in
           10Be(alpha,n)13C reaction were observed with the
           detector array located in the time-of-flight tunnel.
ANALYSIS   Once the neutron peaks in the TOF spectra were
           identified, the 'total counts' or area of each peak
           was determined with a data analysis program.
           Differential cross sections were calculated as
           dsigma(theta,E) = counts/(Ia*nBe*epsilon(En)*dOmega),
           where Ia is the number of incident alpha particles, nBe
            is the thickness of 10Be target (atoms/cm2),
           epsilon(En) is the absolute detector efficiency and
           neutron energy En, dOmega is the solid angle.
CORRECTION Alpha beam was corrected to mid-target position.
           The data from Jim Guilmette have been renormalized
           for the new values for the half-life. The half-life
           used by D.R. Goosman and R.W. Kavanagh, Phys. Rev.
           C 7 1771 (1973) was 1.6 +/- 0.2 My. There have been
           two new measurements of the half-life that closely
           agree. Jerome Chmeleff, Friedhelm von Blanckenburg,
           Karsten Kossert, Dieter Jakob, Nuclear Instrument and
           Methods B 268 192-199 (2010). G. Korschinek, A.
           Bergmaier, T. Faestermann, U.C. Gerstmann, K. Knie,
           G. Rugel, A. Wallner, I. Dillmann, G. Dollinger, Ch.
           Lierse von Gostomski, K. Kossert, M. Maiti, M.
           Poutivtsev, A. Remmert, Nuclear Instruments and
           Methods in Physics Research B 268 (2010) 187-191.
           The new half life was determined as 1.386 +/- 0.016 My.
            This leads to a correction factor of 1.386/1.6 in the
           number of atoms in the target.
STATUS     (APRVD) Approved by T.N.Massey on May 18, 2026.
HISTORY    (20260512C) BP
ENDBIB              58
NOCOMMON             0          0
ENDSUBENT           61
SUBENT        C3128002   20260512   20260819   20260819       C258
BIB                  3          8
REACTION   (4-BE-10(A,N)6-C-13,,DA)
ERR-ANALYS (ERR-T) Uncertainties were added in quadrature's.
           (ERR-S) Statistical uncertainty.
           (ERR-1) Absolute efficiency.
           (ERR-2) Charge collection.
           (ERR-3) Target thickness.
STATUS     (TABLE,,J.F.Guillemette,T,GUILLEMETTE,1994) Data were
           provided by Th.Massey on May 12, 2026.
ENDBIB               8
